---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import PageHeader from '../components/PageHeader.astro';
import SourceList from '../components/SourceList.astro';
import { url } from '../lib/url';

const description =
  'A plain-language explanation of Paroxysmal Nocturnal Haemoglobinuria (PNH): what causes it, its symptoms, how it is diagnosed by flow cytometry, how it is treated, and what access to treatment looks like in India.';
---

<Base title="What is PNH?" description={description}>
  <PageHeader
    eyebrow="Understanding the condition"
    title="What is PNH?"
    lede="Paroxysmal Nocturnal Haemoglobinuria is a rare, acquired blood disorder. This page explains it in ordinary language — what goes wrong, what it feels like, how it is confirmed, what can be done about it, and what getting treated actually involves in India."
  />

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="prose">
        <div class="disclaimer" style="margin-bottom: 2.5rem;">
          <p>
            <strong>This is general information, not medical advice.</strong>
            PNH affects different people very differently. Nothing here should be
            used to make decisions about your own care — bring your questions to a
            haematologist who knows your case. Sources for the clinical and policy
            statements on this page are listed at the bottom.
          </p>
        </div>

        <h2>The name, unpacked</h2>
        <p>
          The name is a mouthful, and it is also slightly misleading — which is
          worth knowing, because it confuses a lot of people early on.
        </p>
        <dl class="definition-list">
          <dt>Paroxysmal</dt>
          <dd>
            "Sudden and irregular." Symptoms were originally thought to come in
            unpredictable attacks. In reality, the underlying process is usually
            continuous, with flare-ups on top.
          </dd>
          <dt>Nocturnal</dt>
          <dd>
            "At night." Named because patients often noticed dark urine first
            thing in the morning. That is mostly because urine is concentrated
            overnight — the destruction of red cells happens around the clock.
          </dd>
          <dt>Haemoglobinuria</dt>
          <dd>
            Haemoglobin — the oxygen-carrying pigment released when red blood
            cells break apart — appearing in the urine, turning it tea-, cola- or
            red-coloured.
          </dd>
        </dl>
        <p>
          So: a condition in which red blood cells break down, releasing
          haemoglobin into the urine. But only about
          <strong>30% of patients ever have visible haemoglobinuria</strong>.
          The majority never see dark urine at all, and its absence does not
          begin to rule PNH out. This single fact is responsible for a great many
          missed diagnoses.
        </p>

        <h2>What actually goes wrong</h2>
        <p>
          Blood cells are made by stem cells in the bone marrow. In PNH, one of
          those stem cells acquires a change in a gene called <em>PIGA</em>. This
          is a <strong>somatic</strong> mutation — it happens in a single cell
          during a person's life. It is not inherited from your parents and you
          cannot pass it to your children.
        </p>
        <p>
          That faulty stem cell copies itself, producing a population of blood
          cells missing a molecular "anchor" that normally holds protective
          proteins onto the cell surface. Two of those missing proteins,
          <strong>CD55</strong> and <strong>CD59</strong>, are the important
          ones. Their job is to hold back the <strong>complement system</strong>
          — an arm of the immune system that destroys bacteria and damaged cells.
        </p>
        <p>
          Without that brake, complement attacks the person's own red blood cells
          and bursts them. This is called <strong>intravascular haemolysis</strong>,
          and nearly everything else about PNH follows from it.
        </p>

        <div class="callout callout--brand">
          <p>
            A useful way to picture it: the complement system is a security
            system that destroys anything without the right ID badge. In PNH,
            some of your own red blood cells are made without a badge. The
            security system is working exactly as designed — it just cannot tell
            that these cells belong to you.
          </p>
        </div>

        <h2>How common is it?</h2>
        <p>
          PNH is rare by any definition. Reported figures vary with how
          populations are screened, but broadly:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>Roughly 1–2 new diagnoses per million people per year</strong>,
            with an estimated prevalence of around <strong>15–16 per million</strong>.
          </li>
          <li>
            It is reported <strong>more frequently across Asia</strong> — including
            Japan, Korea and China — than in Europe or North America.
          </li>
          <li>
            The <strong>median age of onset is in the thirties</strong>, though it
            occurs at any age, and it affects men and women about equally.
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          India has no national PNH registry, so nobody knows how many people
          here are living with it. Applying international prevalence estimates to
          India's population suggests a number in the <em>thousands</em> — while
          the number actually diagnosed and treated is a small fraction of that.
          Closing that gap is a large part of why this Foundation exists.
        </p>

        <h2>Symptoms</h2>
        <p>
          PNH varies enormously between individuals. Only about a third of people
          found to have a PNH clone have "classic" PNH with significant
          haemolysis; people with small clones — generally under 30% — are often
          entirely without symptoms. Common features include:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>Fatigue</strong> — usually the dominant symptom, and often out
            of proportion to the degree of anaemia. Many patients describe it as
            the hardest part of the condition to live with, and the hardest to
            explain to other people.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Anaemia</strong> — breathlessness, pallor, palpitations,
            dizziness, poor concentration.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Dark urine</strong> — most noticeable in the morning, and
            present in only about 30% of patients.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Blood clots (thrombosis)</strong> — the most serious
            complication of PNH, and historically its leading cause of death.
            Clots can form in unusual places, including the veins draining the
            abdomen and the brain. Sudden severe abdominal pain, a swollen painful
            leg, chest pain or breathlessness need <strong>urgent</strong>
            medical attention.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Abdominal pain, difficulty swallowing, and erectile
            dysfunction</strong> — caused by smooth muscle spasm, a knock-on
            effect of free haemoglobin mopping up nitric oxide in the blood. These
            are genuine PNH symptoms and worth raising with your doctor even
            though they can be embarrassing to bring up.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Kidney problems</strong> and <strong>raised pressure in the
            lungs</strong> can develop over time with sustained haemolysis.
          </li>
        </ul>

        <h2>PNH, aplastic anaemia and bone marrow failure</h2>
        <p>
          PNH is closely linked to <strong>aplastic anaemia</strong>, a condition
          in which the bone marrow fails to produce enough blood cells. Around
          <strong>half of all people with acquired aplastic anaemia have a
          detectable PNH clone</strong>, and classic PNH can evolve out of
          aplastic anaemia over time.
        </p>
        <p>
          Because of this, anyone with aplastic anaemia or unexplained low blood
          counts should be tested for PNH, and people with aplastic anaemia are
          generally re-screened annually. This connection matters practically in
          India, where aplastic anaemia is comparatively well recognised while PNH
          is not — the aplastic anaemia diagnosis is often the door through which
          a PNH diagnosis eventually arrives.
        </p>

        <h2>Why diagnosis is so often delayed</h2>
        <p>
          Delays of years between first symptoms and a confirmed diagnosis are
          common. One Indian case series found a
          <strong>mean delay of 3.7 years</strong> from symptom onset, with
          patients labelled along the way with iron-deficiency anaemia, haemolytic
          anaemia, megaloblastic or refractory anaemia, myelodysplastic syndrome,
          or simply "haematuria". The reasons are structural, not anyone's fault:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>PNH is rare enough that most clinicians never encounter a case.</li>
          <li>
            Its symptoms — tiredness, anaemia, abdominal pain — are extremely
            common and point to many other things first.
          </li>
          <li>
            Most patients never have the dark urine the disease is named after,
            so the one memorable clue is usually missing.
          </li>
          <li>
            Patients are often treated for iron-deficiency anaemia for a long
            time before anyone asks why the anaemia is not responding.
          </li>
          <li>
            The confirmatory test is not part of a routine workup; someone has to
            specifically think of PNH and order it.
          </li>
        </ul>

        <div class="callout">
          <h3>When PNH testing should be considered</h3>
          <p>
            International practice is to test for a PNH clone in anyone with
            unexplained haemolytic anaemia (particularly with a raised LDH and a
            negative Coombs test), unexplained cytopenias or aplastic anaemia, or
            a thrombosis in an unusual site — especially in the veins of the
            abdomen or brain — without a clear cause.
          </p>
          <p>
            If that describes you, it is entirely reasonable to ask your doctor
            directly: <em>"Should I be tested for PNH?"</em>
          </p>
        </div>

        <h2>How PNH is diagnosed</h2>
        <p>
          The definitive test is <strong>high-sensitivity flow cytometry</strong>
          on a sample of peripheral blood. The laboratory examines the surface of
          your blood cells and measures what proportion are missing CD55 and
          CD59, usually together with a reagent called
          <abbr title="Fluorescently labelled aerolysin">FLAER</abbr>, which
          improves accuracy on white blood cells.
        </p>
        <p>
          Done to international (ICCS/ESCCA) standards, the test can detect clones
          down to about 0.01% of red cells — extraordinarily sensitive. The result
          is reported as the size of your <strong>PNH clone</strong>: the
          percentage of your blood cells affected. That number matters. It
          influences whether you need treatment and how closely you are monitored,
          and it is rechecked over time, along with
          <abbr title="Lactate dehydrogenase — a marker of red cell destruction">LDH</abbr>
          as a measure of how much haemolysis is occurring.
        </p>
        <p>Two practical points patients often ask about:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>It is a blood test, not a bone marrow biopsy.</strong> A
            marrow test may be done separately to look for aplastic anaemia or
            other marrow conditions, but it is not how PNH itself is confirmed.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Older tests are obsolete.</strong> The Ham test and sucrose
            lysis test have been replaced by flow cytometry, which is far more
            sensitive. If a laboratory offers you one of these, ask about flow
            cytometry instead.
          </li>
        </ul>

        <h2>How PNH is treated</h2>
        <p>
          Treatment depends on clone size, how much haemolysis is happening,
          symptoms, clot history, and whether there is bone marrow failure
          alongside. <strong>Not everyone with a PNH clone needs drug
          treatment</strong> — people with small clones and little haemolysis are
          often monitored rather than treated.
        </p>

        <h3>Complement inhibitors</h3>
        <p>
          These are the disease-modifying treatments, and they changed the outlook
          for PNH fundamentally. They block the complement system so it can no
          longer destroy the unprotected red cells. Several classes now exist:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>C5 inhibitors</strong> — eculizumab, ravulizumab and
            crovalimab. The longest-established approach, blocking complement at a
            late ("terminal") step. Given by infusion or injection.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Proximal inhibitors</strong> — pegcetacoplan (C3), iptacopan
            (Factor B) and danicopan (Factor D). These act earlier in the
            pathway, and can address <em>extravascular</em> haemolysis, a
            limitation that emerges in some patients on C5 inhibitors alone.
            Iptacopan and danicopan are taken orally rather than infused.
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          Which of these is appropriate — and which are actually obtainable in
          India — are different questions. The section below covers the second.
        </p>

        <div class="callout callout--alert">
          <h3>An important safety point</h3>
          <p>
            Blocking complement raises the risk of serious infection,
            particularly <strong>meningococcal disease</strong>. Anyone starting a
            complement inhibitor should be vaccinated against meningococcus
            <strong>at least 14 days beforehand</strong>, or started on preventive
            antibiotics if treatment cannot wait, and should carry a patient alert
            card. If you are on one of these drugs and develop a fever, treat it
            as urgent and seek medical help immediately — do not wait to see if it
            settles.
          </p>
        </div>

        <h3>Supportive care</h3>
        <p>
          Alongside or instead of complement inhibitors, care may include blood
          transfusions, folic acid and iron supplementation, and anticoagulation
          to prevent or treat clots. For patients who cannot access complement
          inhibitors — still the majority in India — this supportive care
          <em>is</em> the treatment, and it matters a great deal.
        </p>

        <h3>Bone marrow transplant</h3>
        <p>
          An allogeneic stem cell transplant is the only treatment that can cure
          PNH, but it carries substantial risks of its own. It is generally
          reserved for people with severe bone marrow failure, or where other
          treatment is not an option. In India it also carries a particular
          significance: where complement inhibitors are unaffordable, transplant
          is sometimes discussed earlier than it would be elsewhere. It is a
          decision to work through carefully with a transplant centre.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- The India-specific section: the reason this site exists -->
  <section class="section section--muted">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="prose">
        <span class="eyebrow">The part most websites leave out</span>
        <h2 id="pnh-in-india">PNH in India: what access actually looks like</h2>
        <p>
          Almost all PNH information online is written for the United States or
          Europe, where the treatments described above are broadly available. In
          India the clinical picture is the same but the access picture is not,
          and being told about a drug you cannot obtain is its own kind of cruelty.
          Here is the honest position.
        </p>

        <h3>Eculizumab is now approved in India</h3>
        <p>
          In <strong>January 2025</strong>, the Central Drugs Standard Control
          Organisation (CDSCO) approved eculizumab for import and distribution in
          India for PNH and atypical haemolytic uraemic syndrome, in both adults
          and children. It was the
          <strong>first anti-complement therapy approved in the country</strong>,
          and it matters: before that, treatment meant named-patient import
          arrangements or nothing.
        </p>
        <p>
          Approval, however, is not the same as access. Complement inhibitors are
          among the most expensive medicines in the world, and the cost of
          sustained treatment remains out of reach for the overwhelming majority
          of Indian families without structured financial support. Published
          Indian case series describe cohorts managed
          <em>entirely without</em> C5 inhibitors — which tells you plainly where
          things stand.
        </p>

        <h3>The rare disease policy gap</h3>
        <p>
          India's <strong>National Policy for Rare Diseases (NPRD), 2021</strong>
          provides financial assistance of up to <strong>₹50 lakh per patient</strong>
          for treatment at a designated <strong>Centre of Excellence</strong>.
          The network has grown from 8 CoEs to 15, including AIIMS Delhi as
          coordinating centre, PGIMER Chandigarh, CMC Vellore, AIIMS Bhopal, AIIMS
          Patna, RIMS Imphal and Assam Medical College, among others.
        </p>

        <div class="callout callout--alert">
          <h3>PNH is not currently on the NPRD list</h3>
          <p>
            The policy covers <strong>63 named rare diseases</strong> across its
            three groups. Reviewing that list as published, <strong>Paroxysmal
            Nocturnal Haemoglobinuria does not appear on it</strong> — and neither
            does aplastic anaemia. In practical terms this means PNH patients
            cannot presently claim the ₹50 lakh grant on the basis of their PNH
            diagnosis, even though the condition is rare, has a definitive
            treatment, and carries exactly the lifelong high-cost profile the
            policy's Group 3 was written to address.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Getting PNH recognised under the NPRD is the single most
            consequential policy change for patients in India</strong>, and it is
            a central goal of this Foundation.
          </p>
          <p class="verify-note">
            Verified against the published NPRD disease list on 28 July 2026. The
            list is revised periodically on the recommendation of the Central
            Technical Committee for Rare Diseases, so please confirm the current
            position before relying on it — and
            <a href={url('/contact')}>tell us</a> if you find it has changed.
          </p>
        </div>

        <h3>What this means for you in practice</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>Get to a centre that has seen PNH before.</strong> The
            Centres of Excellence and large teaching hospitals concentrate both
            the diagnostic capability — high-sensitivity flow cytometry with FLAER
            is not universally available — and the clinical experience.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Ask about every funding route, not just one.</strong>
            State-level schemes, hospital charity funds, manufacturer patient
            assistance programmes and CSR-funded support all exist and operate
            independently of the NPRD. Eligibility varies by state and changes
            often.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Insist on a properly performed flow cytometry report.</strong>
            Clone size in both red cells and granulocytes, reported to
            international standards, is the document everything else depends on.
            Keep a copy.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Supportive care is real care.</strong> If complement
            inhibitors are not currently within reach, transfusion support, folic
            acid, anticoagulation where indicated, and vigilant monitoring for
            thrombosis remain genuinely effective at protecting life and quality
            of life.
          </li>
        </ul>

        <div class="todo-note">
          TODO (Foundation): this section is the site's most valuable asset and
          the fastest to go stale. Before launch, have a treating haematologist
          verify it, and re-check at least every six months: (a) whether PNH has
          been added to the NPRD list; (b) which complement inhibitors beyond
          eculizumab have since received CDSCO approval; (c) the current CoE list
          and count; (d) live patient assistance programmes. Update the `checked`
          dates in src/data/sources.ts whenever you do.
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="prose">
        <h2>Living with PNH</h2>
        <p>
          PNH is a long-term condition, and living well with it involves more than
          drugs: pacing yourself around fatigue, knowing which symptoms are
          emergencies, managing work and family expectations, and dealing with the
          mental weight of a rare diagnosis. Our
          <a href={url('/patient-support')}>patient support page</a> covers the
          practical side, and we can put you in touch with others living with the
          same condition.
        </p>

        <div class="btn-row">
          <a href={url('/patient-support')} class="btn btn--primary">Patient support</a>
          <a href={url('/resources')} class="btn btn--secondary">Trusted resources</a>
          <a href={url('/contact')} class="btn btn--secondary">Ask us a question</a>
        </div>

        <SourceList
          ids={[
            'merck',
            'nord',
            'iccs',
            'slr-2026',
            'cdsco-eculizumab',
            'nprd',
            'nprd-list',
            'coe',
            'india-cohort',
            'india-flaer',
          ]}
        />
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>

